{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0133JcNiGfNqpeiQiA4vyJJT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the research data carefully to define three tiers of scenarios for this VLA-SSD paper targeting CoRL 2026.\n\nThe key questions from the focus are:\n1. C2 cross-architecture validation (3 arch × 4 suite) — is it enough to address RC4?\n2. D090 training-induced vs base-model-dependent distinction — strengthens or weakens story?\n3. D094 three-tier routing gradient as new finding\n4. R² exit collapse as architecture-independent signature\n5. Overall CoRL accept readiness\n\nLet me look at the actual data:\n\n**R² Exit Collapse (the strongest finding):**\n\n\nThe data shows trained models have dramatically larger R² drops compared to untrained ones across nearly all cases, with the exception of OpenVLA Long. The trained deltas span a much wider range than untrained deltas, which stay relatively small and consistent.\n\n**Counterfactual Sensitivity:**\nTrained models show 7-10× higher sensitivity than untrained across the board, and the \"empty\" intervention consistently ranks as the most impactful in trained settings.\n\n**L1 Heads (D090 — base-model-dependent):**\nTracVLA already shows maximal head activation in the untrained state, so training actually reduces the count slightly rather than inducing it.\n\n**Three-tier routing gradient (D094):**\nThere's a clear progression: VLM bases show no routing collapse or counterfactual effects, OXE-pretrained models develop strong routing and sensitivity without exit collapse, and task-finetuned models exhibit both exit collapse and task-specific counterfactual patterns.\n\n**Remaining gaps/risks:**\nNORA's limited suite coverage, the OpenVLA Long outlier, the pending ablation rerun, the failed behavioral validation, and the divergence between vision encoder metrics all point to areas where the mechanistic picture is still incomplete or inconsistent.\n\nA few OpenVLA standardized experiments are still running, and once those finish I can assess whether the cross-architecture pattern holds. The best outcome is that everything validates cleanly and the three-tier gradient emerges as a genuine contribution—the distinction between training-induced and base-model-dependent effects would then read as honest insight rather than a limitation. The median scenario is messier: the pattern holds but with noise, the OFT outlier creates doubt, and reviewers might see the training distinction as confusing rather than clarifying.\n\nIn the worst case, reviewers dismiss this as trivial (of course training changes representations) and point out that zero closed-loop success undermines any practical claims. The fragmented story—some effects are training-induced, some aren't, and we can't always tell which—would feel post-hoc rather than principled. I need to define concrete metrics now to track which tier we're actually in: R² exit collapse consistency across cells, the ratio of sensitivity in trained versus untrained models, and whether cross-architecture R² peaks align across architectures.\n\nI'm also realizing the remaining experiments are still running, so this \"simulation\" is really about how reviewers will interpret the current evidence and what those in-progress results might reveal. The key unknowns that will shift the outcome are still being collected.\n\nFor the tiers, I should anchor on concrete metrics: R² collapse differences between trained and exit states, counterfactual sensitivity ratios, and how consistently the peak layer appears across different architectures. The reviewer interpretation hinges on whether the cross-architecture patterns hold up and if the narrative about OFT as an outlier feels justified. are largely done. The \"dream simulation\" tiers should be about:\n\n1. What happens with the 2 remaining RUNNING experiments\n2. How the paper holds up under review — which reviewer objections materialize and how severe they are\n3. Whether the narrative framing survives scrutiny\n\nNow I'm looking at the specific metrics from the data. The R² exit collapse values show a clear pattern, with NORA Goal standing out at 0.691 while most others cluster lower. The counterfactual sensitivity measurements reveal similar trends across the models, with NORA Goal again saturating at cosine distance 1.0. The two pending experiments should fill in the OpenVLA Goal and Spatial rows, completing most of the 3×4 matrix and either confirming the exit collapse pattern or revealing unexpected variation.\n\nFor the best-case scenario, those running experiments would show OpenVLA Goal and Spatial exhibiting the same clear exit collapse pattern, giving me 11-12 confirmed cells out of 12 and a cohesive narrative for the paper.\n\nIn the median case, the effects would be weaker — maybe the exit collapse shows up but less dramatically — leaving some cells unexplained and giving reviewers room to question whether the three-tier gradient is truly novel or just an incremental refinement of the trained/untrained distinction. The OFT outlier would still hang there unanswered, and someone would inevitably ask whether any of this matters behaviorally without closed-loop validation.\n\nIn the worst case, a reviewer dismisses the R² collapse as a trivial artifact of representation compression rather than something specific to VLA training, and the lack of behavioral validation becomes the killing blow — the representational story just doesn't connect to actual task performance. The three-tier gradient gets written off as post-hoc cherry-picking, and the whole narrative collapses.\n\nNow I'm setting concrete thresholds to distinguish these scenarios: for the best case, I need at least 11 out of 12 cells showing trained collapse larger than untrained, with mean deltas in the 0.15–0.30 range and a trained-to-untrained sensitivity ratio of at least 5× on the counterfactual analysis.\n\nFor the median outcome, I'm looking at 8–10 cells confirming the pattern, smaller deltas around 0.08–0.20, and sensitivity ratios between 3–8×. The worst case drops to 6–8 cells with weak deltas under 0.12 and ratios mostly below 5×, dragged down by OpenVLA underperformance.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what assumptions would actually drive each tier. The best case hinges on running OpenVLA experiments that confirm the exit collapse effect, a convincing explanation for the OFT outlier, and reviewers accepting the architecture-independence claim across three models. The median tier assumes more moderate experimental results, while the worst case would involve weaker confirmations and less compelling explanations for the outliers.\n\nNow I need to define concrete, measurable metrics for the YAML output...\n\nLooking at what actually matters for this paper: the count of cells showing exit collapse, the magnitude of the trained-versus-untrained difference, how consistently the pattern holds across different architectures, and whether the three-tier gradient structure stays intact. I should also capture how many of the core claims can survive peer review and how well the narrative holds together under scrutiny.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what each tier really means. The best case is when all three main effects—the R² collapse, the CF sensitivity shift, and the three-tier gradient—replicate cleanly across architectures, making this a strong accept with three distinct contributions. The middle ground is when the primary effect holds but secondary patterns get muddier, especially around the D090 distinction and the tier-two validation, which would make this borderline depending on reviewer fit. The worst case is when the architecture-independence claim starts to crack under scrutiny.\n\nThe next tier down faces real challenges: the FT outlier, NORA's incomplete coverage, and weak effects in certain cells all undermine the core narrative. Reviewers might question whether representational analysis alone justifies a robotics venue publication without behavioral grounding. The framing would need to shift from \"VLA training creates routing\" to something more measured, though publication is still possible with substantial revision.\n\nNow I'm settling on concrete metrics that are already partially measured and can meaningfully differentiate the tiers. I'm looking at how many of the tested cells actually show the R² collapse, the average magnitude of that collapse across architectures, and the CF ratio patterns.\n\nDefining metrics for both evidence strength and reviewer reception...\n\nNow I'm thinking through what the running experiments will likely show—OpenVLA on Goal and Spatial should follow similar patterns to what we've already seen with Object, though the magnitudes might differ. I have enough grounding to write out the YAML configuration.\n\nFor the paper strategy, I need to map out three scenarios: if everything holds, I can keep the original framing about VLA training creating architecture-independent routing reorganization; if results are more mixed, I'll need to emphasize R² exit collapse as the most robust finding and present the other routing aspects as inherited from the base model; and if things fall apart, I pivot to positioning the empirical framework itself as the main contribution, with the findings as illustrative examples. Now I'm ready to construct the YAML.\n\nBut I should also consider that CoRL reviewers specifically care about practical robotics relevance and closed-loop behavioral validation—not just representational analysis. The lack of real robot task validation is a real vulnerability for this venue, so I need to factor that into how I frame each tier's viability.\n\nWriting the configuration YAML... Looking at the performance metrics and ranges for the paper's acceptance likelihood, I'm seeing the R² collapse confirmation sits around 85-95% across cells, with trained exit deltas between 0.15-0.30, and the cross-architecture evidence spanning 10-12 out of 12 matrix completeness points. The narrative holds—all three contributions land cleanly: the systematic framework, the architecture-independent training signatures through R² collapse and CF sensitivity, and the three-tier routing gradient structure, with the D090 distinction positioned as a nuanced finding rather than a core claim. I'm weighing the evidence on what training actually creates versus what emerges from the architecture itself—reviewers are pushing back on whether the L1 head effects are truly training-induced, and they want behavioral or closed-loop validation. The three-tier gradient feels underpowered with only one Tier-2 data point, and the vision encoder geometry claims are weakened by conflicting RSA/CKA results, so I'm estimating the R² collapse holds across 67-83% of cells with trained-untrained ratios ranging from 3.5 to 7.0.\n\nI need to soften the language around architecture-independence and move the gradient analysis from a core claim into the discussion section, while reframing the main finding as R² collapse being the robust signature with L1 routing reflecting pre-existing language capacity. I should add explicit limitations acknowledging the behavioral gap and the OFT outlier, and further minimize the vision encoder contribution.\n\nFor the worst-case scenario, I'm considering how reviewers might challenge the cross-architecture evidence as insufficient or trivial, criticize the behavioral gap harshly, and question whether exit collapse is just a generic decoder-only compression effect rather than VLA-specific. The lack of behavioral validation could be seen as fatal for a robotics venue, and if the experiments show weak results or inconsistency across architectures, the paper's core argument collapses. Looking at the numerical ranges and acceptance probabilities, I'm realizing the core hypothesis doesn't hold up—I need to pivot the framing entirely. Instead of claiming VLA training creates routing signatures, I should position this as a diagnostic framework for analyzing how different architectures represent internal states, making the methodological contribution the centerpiece. The R² collapse and sensitivity findings become evidence that the framework reveals architectural differences rather than universal properties, and I can repackage the D090 distinction as a framework discovery showing what's pre-existing versus training-induced. I'm planning to add a couple more analyses to strengthen this reframed narrative.\n\nNow I'm double-checking that my metric ranges are internally consistent—verifying that best estimates exceed medians which exceed worst-case across all measurements. I'm noticing some boundary overlaps between tiers, but they're actually reasonable—they reflect uncertainty at the edges rather than hard cutoffs. The concern is whether the best-case mean exit delta range of 0.15 to 0.30 is grounded in the actual data, so I'm checking against the observed values from the different model variants.\n\nLooking at the confirmed cells, the mean comes out to about 0.282, which suggests the best case should probably sit around 0.20 to 0.35 rather than starting as low as 0.15. When we report the honest mean that includes weaker cells, the worst case has both new experiments underperforming, which shifts the narrative weight toward OFT and brings the effective mean down to around 0.10-0.18. Let me ground these ranges more realistically: best case lands between 0.22-0.32, median between 0.12-0.22, and worst case between 0.05-0.14, which maintains the proper ordering with acceptable boundary overlap. Now I'm checking the CF ratio — looking at the OpenVLA Object baseline where the trained model shows d=1.21 but the untrained cosine similarity is 0.569, which doesn't directly map to what I need.\n\nComparing across different cell types, TracVLA shows d values of 1.2-1.5 for trained versus 0.15-0.19 cosine for untrained, suggesting a ratio around 7-10x if I normalize the metrics, while NORA reaches about 12x with its 1.0 trained versus 0.081 untrained cosine. The real issue is that I'm mixing Cohen's d with cosine distance, so let me anchor to the framing document's stated \"trained 7-10× untrained\" and use that as my reference point. I'll set best case at 7-12x, median at 4-8x to account for weaker cells, and worst case at 2-5x for the noisier results. These ranges feel grounded now. For the r2_collapse_confirmed_fraction, I have 9 out of 10 tested cells confirming the pattern, but I need to check what happens with the remaining data.\n\nLooking at the actual matrix, TracVLA covers all four suites (Goal, Spatial, Object, Long), OpenVLA has Object and Long, and NORA only has Goal and Spatial, so I'm working with 8 cells total for the R² collapse analysis plus the original OpenVLA data. I'm recalibrating the confidence ranges based on what's actually at stake here. Most cells are already confirmed, so the real uncertainty isn't about existing results changing—it's whether the new OpenVLA experiments land well and whether reviewers accept the marginal cases. The worst case is really just 7 cells being solidly confirmed with the OFT outlier and 2 weak new results, which puts the effective confirmation rate around 0.50-0.70.\n\nNow I need to clarify what I'm actually measuring with matrix_completeness—it should reflect how many cells have the full pipeline of data (R², L1 norm, cross-instance, and attention probes), not just R² collapse alone. Looking at what's currently available, I have full coverage for TracVLA Goal, TracVLA Long, and NORA Goal, with partial data scattered across the other cells. So I should focus on counting cells with R² exit collapse specifically rather than trying to track the complete pipeline.\n\nI'm going to write out the final YAML structure now, though I'm realizing I should add some more targeted metrics that directly connect to the paper's core claims—things like whether the three-tier ordering (Tier1 < Tier2 < Tier3) holds cleanly, how much weight the C3 contribution carries, and whether the training-induced versus base-model-dependent distinction reads as a feature or limitation. These are inherently qualitative, so I'll keep the quantitative metrics focused on what I can measure and add narrative descriptions for the rest.\n\nFor the mean exit delta calculation, I should base it on the cells I've already confirmed rather than speculating about new ones—that puts me around 0.25, and even with optimistic new data, I'm probably looking at a modest increase from there. The boundary overlaps are fine for probabilistic tiers, so I'll move forward with writing out the YAML configuration.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":49538,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":49538},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4078766-e775-4f63-8a0f-2768669ef059","uuid":"1ba74df5-8f0c-412e-aec4-83b8898416a6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0133JcNiGfNqpeiQiA4vyJJT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"跨架构验证全面成立，D090 区分被视为科学严谨性体现，三层级梯度被接受为新贡献，CoRL accept\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"RUNNING 的 OpenVLA Goal/Spatial 实验确认 exit collapse（trained Δ > 0.08）\"\n      - \"OFT outlier 被接受为 parameter-efficient finetuning 特例，不影响主 claim\"\n      - \"3 arch × 10 cells 被视为足够的跨架构证据\"\n      - \"D090 training-induced vs base-model-dependent 区分增强而非削弱可信度\"\n      - \"representational analysis 在 CoRL 被接受为独立贡献，closed-loop gap 不致命\"\n      - \"三层级梯度 (VLM→OXE-VLA→task-VLA) 被视为 genuine new finding\"\n    number_ranges:\n      r2_collapse_confirmed_cells: [9, 10]        # out of 10 testable cells\n      mean_trained_exit_delta: [0.20, 0.35]        # R² (peak−exit), averaged across confirmed cells\n      cf_sensitivity_ratio: [7, 12]                # trained/untrained effect size ratio (median across cells)\n      matrix_cells_with_full_data: [9, 10]         # cells with R²+CF+attention complete\n      cross_arch_r2_peak_layer_std: [1.5, 3.0]    # layers, lower=more consistent\n      expected_reviewer_avg_score: [5.5, 7.0]      # CoRL 1-10 scale\n      accept_probability: [0.55, 0.75]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      D095 framing 完全成立。标题保持 \"architecture-independent routing reorganization\"。\n      三个贡献全部 land: (1) systematic token-level framework validated on 3 arch × 4 suites,\n      (2) R² exit collapse + CF sensitivity 作为 architecture-independent training signatures,\n      (3) three-tier routing gradient (emerge at VLM→VLA, reshape at general→task-specific)。\n      D090 区分作为 nuanced finding 呈现: \"we rigorously distinguish what training creates\n      (exit collapse, CF sensitivity) from what it inherits (L1 head patterns)\"。\n      Abstract/claims 保持当前强度，无需降级。C3 保持 qualified observation。\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"R² collapse 跨架构大体成立但有噪声，D090 引起混合反应，三层级梯度被降为 observation，borderline accept\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"RUNNING 实验中 1 个确认 collapse、1 个效应偏弱（Δ < 0.05）\"\n      - \"OFT + 1-2 个弱 cell 削弱 'architecture-independent' 的绝对性\"\n      - \"至少 1 个 reviewer 质疑 D090: 'L1 不是训练效果，那训练到底贡献了什么？'\"\n      - \"至少 1 个 reviewer 要求 behavioral/closed-loop 证据\"\n      - \"三层级梯度因仅 1 个 Tier-2 数据点被视为 suggestive 而非 validated\"\n      - \"NORA 仅 2/4 suites 被视为 incomplete coverage\"\n    number_ranges:\n      r2_collapse_confirmed_cells: [7, 9]\n      mean_trained_exit_delta: [0.10, 0.22]\n      cf_sensitivity_ratio: [4, 8]\n      matrix_cells_with_full_data: [7, 9]\n      cross_arch_r2_peak_layer_std: [3.0, 5.0]\n      expected_reviewer_avg_score: [4.5, 5.5]\n      accept_probability: [0.25, 0.45]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      调整 claim 强度。\"Architecture-independent\" 改为 \"consistent across three tested\n      architectures\"，承认 sample size 限制。三层级梯度从 core finding 降为 Discussion\n      observation (\"suggestive of a routing gradient, requiring validation with more\n      OXE-pretrained checkpoints\")。D090 需要更清晰的 positive framing: \"R² exit collapse\n      is the robust cross-architecture signature; L1 head count reflects inherited language\n      capacity\"。增加显式 Limitations 段: behavioral validation gap, OFT outlier, NORA\n      incomplete matrix, single Tier-2 datapoint。C3 压缩到 appendix。可能补充 1 个轻量\n      additional analysis（已有 features、无新 GPU 成本）增强 contribution density。\n      仍可 accept 但需要 reviewer champion。\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"跨架构证据被质疑为不充分或 trivial，behavioral gap 被严厉批评，需要 reframe 论文角度但不降 venue\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"RUNNING 实验结果弱或不一致（OpenVLA Spatial 无明显 exit collapse）\"\n      - \"Reviewer 论证 exit collapse 可能是 decoder-only 架构的通用表征压缩，非 VLA-specific\"\n      - \"Behavioral validation 缺失在 CoRL（robotics venue）被视为 near-fatal weakness\"\n      - \"D090 被解读为 'VLA 训练的独特效果只剩 exit collapse 一个，contribution 太薄'\"\n      - \"三层级梯度被驳为 post-hoc narrative（仅 1 个 Tier-2 点无法建立 gradient）\"\n      - \"至少 2 个 reviewer 给 reject，reason 集中在实用价值和 claim 强度